      "text": "**Problem:**\nFind the distance between the points $(-3, -4)$ and $(4, -5)$.\n\nTo solve this problem, we will use the distance formula. The distance formula for two points $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ in a Cartesian plane is given by:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points $(-3, -4)$ and $(4, -5)$. \n\nSteps:\n1. Identify the coordinates $(x_1, y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$:\n   \\[\n   (x_1, y_1) = (-3, -4)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (x_2, y_2) = (4, -5)\n   \\]\n\n2. Substitute these coordinates into the distance formula:\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify the expression inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{(4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{49 + 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{50}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = 5\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the distance between the points $(-3, -4)$ and $(4, -5)$ is $5\\sqrt{2}$.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
