      "text": "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nGiven the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\), we identify:\n\\[\n(x_1, y_1) = (-3, -4)\n\\]\n\\[\n(x_2, y_2) = (4, -5)\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute these values into the distance formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n-5 - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these simplified values back into the formula:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the squares:\n\\[\n7^2 = 49\n\\]\n\\[\n(-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nAdd these values together:\n\\[\n49 + 1 = 50\n\\]\n\nFinally, take the square root of the sum:\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{50}\n\\]\n\nSimplify \\(\\sqrt{50}\\) by factoring it into a product of a perfect square and another factor:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{25} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
